  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How do earthworms help soil?",
      "a": "They mix dead leaves into the soil, break them down, leave nutrient-rich casts and dig tunnels that let in air and water. Plants usually grow better where there are plenty of worms.",
      "asks": [
        "Are worms good for the garden?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are worm casts?",
      "a": "Casts are worm droppings: soil and leaf bits that have passed through the worm. They are crumbly and richer in nutrients like nitrogen and phosphorus than the surrounding soil.",
      "asks": [
        "What are the little piles on the lawn?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are worm burrows good for soil?",
      "a": "Burrows let air reach roots and soil microbes, let rain soak in instead of running off, and give roots easy paths to grow down.",
      "asks": [
        "How do worms let air into soil?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do worms come up when it rains?",
      "a": "Scientists are not completely sure. Wet ground lets worms travel across the surface without drying out, and some think the vibration of raindrops or low oxygen in soaked soil play a part.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are there worms on the path after rain?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What did Darwin discover about worms?",
      "a": "In his last book, in 1881, Darwin showed that worms slowly bury stones and old ruins by piling casts on top, and that they turn over huge amounts of soil every year.",
      "asks": [
        "Why did Darwin study worms?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Charles Darwin's last book, published in 1881, was about earthworms and how they make soil.",
    "Worm casts are richer in plant nutrients than the soil around them.",
    "Earthworms pull fallen leaves down into their burrows, mixing dead plant matter into the soil."
